{ https://youtu.be/VAHDgpJTJ4w?si=gEsHtkYai8NFQArk&t=18368 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] Against e4, Aman goes for this French Defence move in the hope of getting a Stonewall setup. } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:58] This is good for us, because in the Stonewall we want to open the e-file.
Note that if White had played e5 here (i.e. transposing into the French Advanced Variation) then Aman would have abandoned the Stonewall and just played French Defence moves. } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:53] A sub-optimal move (blocking the d-pawn). In later videos, Aman will face tougher competition. } f5 { [%clk 0:04:50] Continuing with the Stonewall plan. } (4... Bd6 5. O-O Ne7 6. Re1 Nc6 7. Nc3 Bg4 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Qd7 10. Bf5 { our attempts to play the French exchange, aggressive version, can fall into troubles in this line. }) 5. O-O { [%clk 0:04:52] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 6. Re1+ { [%clk 0:04:51] } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:47] } (6... Ne4 7. Nc3 c6 8. Bxe4 fxe4 9. d3 Bd6 10. dxe4 O-O { this is losing for us apparently. So there are times when we can play an early Ne4 in response to a check on the e file and times when it doesn't work. }) 7. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:43] } (7. Qe2 Ne4 8. c4 O-O 9. cxd5 Bc5 10. Bxe4 fxe4 11. Qxe4 { Opponent could have played this stronger. Always hard to force the stonewall to e4 as black. }) 7... O-O { [%clk 0:04:44] } 8. Qf3 { [%clk 0:04:33] Adding pressure to the f5 pawn. } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:40] This standard Stonewall move solves the problem, by blocking the bishop's attack on the pawn, and uncovering The Rf8's defense of it. } 9. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:27] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:37] As (almost) always, we take with the f-pawn here. } 10. Qg3 { [%clk 0:04:16] } Bh4 { [%clk 0:04:26] This actually a skewer on the f2 pawn. } (10... Nd7) 11. Qb3 { [%clk 0:03:48] } Bxf2+ { [%clk 0:04:22] } 12. Kf1 { [%clk 0:03:39] } Bd4+ { [%clk 0:03:59] Aman could have taken the rook here, but that would have only won him the exchange.
With this move, he is winning a whole piece. } 13. Ke2 { [%clk 0:03:33] } (13. Nf3 exf3 14. gxf3 Bh3+ 15. Ke2 Re8+ 16. Kd1 Rxe1+ 17. Kxe1 Qh4+ 18. Kd1 Qh5 { Opponent could have blocked the check with his knight but that was losing too. }) 13... Rf2+ { [%clk 0:03:56] } 14. Kd1 { [%clk 0:03:31] Before taking the N, Aman senses a checkmate is in the air. } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:53] Let's take this N first before proceeding. } 15. d3 { [%clk 0:03:23] } Bg4+ { [%clk 0:03:51] } 16. Re2 { [%clk 0:03:10] White resigned here (there is actually a forced checkmate here). } Rf1+ 17. Kd2 e3+ 18. Rxe3 (18. Kxe3 Qg5#) 18... Rd1# { 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

