{ youtu.be/VAHDgpJTJ4w?si=I1HcxdcvO2F3gCJ9&t=20849 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3. c4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 4. c5 { [%clk 0:04:55] We are always happy to see this move. It is a one move threat that achieves nothing. We can just drop our bishop back. Meanwhile, the tension on the d pawn is lost. It makes it more likely that we can push e5 in the future when the timing is right, to remove our weaknesses and free our position. } (4. cxd5 exd5 { on takes, we will always try to take back with our weak backwards e pawn. In the SW we have two major weaknesses right out of the opening: 1: Our weak backwards e pawn. 2: Our LSB (with black) that is trapped behind the e pawn. Our plans often involve killing two birds with one stone, for example, by pushing e5, and on cxe5, we take back Nxe5 and get rid of our pawn weakness while freeing our LSB. }) 4... f5 { [%clk 0:04:51] As we've seen before, Aman sometimes sets up his pawns first, before moving any pieces. } 5. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:47] } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:35] Aman preferred this to Nf6, because he was worried that White might then trade off his important Stonewall knight. Also, trading off our DS bishop is no great loss, since the pawn on c5 is restricting its development. } 6. Qa4?? { [%clk 0:04:21] This is a simple counting blunder by White, which loses a piece. } (6. Bxe7 Qxe7 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. e3 O-O 9. Be2 Bd7 10. O-O Be8 11. b4 Nbd7 12. Rc1 Bh5 13. Ne5 Bxe2 14. Qxe2 Nxe5 15. dxe5 Ne4 16. Nxe4 fxe4 { In the post game analysis Aman talks about how we get an imbalance playing the SW as black in that when our opponent tries to get a knight into e5 and we get ours into e4 after both are captured we get better pawn structure. Our pawns are connected by our opponent has isolated pawns. Also we have the semi open f file to work with. If our opponent plays f4 we will take en passant. We will put pressure on his isolated pawn with Qg5... Rf5 etc., and there is not such our opponent can do about it. } 17. f4 exf3 18. Rxf3 Qg5 19. Rcf1 Qxe5 { winning the pawn. }) 6... Bxg5 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 7. Nxg5 { [%clk 0:04:21] } Qxg5 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 8. e3 { [%clk 0:04:17] } (8. Qa5 Qc1# { great way to lose the game }) (8. Qb4 Qc1# { great way to lose the game }) 8... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:21] } 9. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:16] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:18] } 10. O-O-O { [%clk 0:04:05] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 11. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:01] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:05] } 12. g4 { [%clk 0:03:40] } Rxf2 { [%clk 0:04:00] } (12... Qxg4) 13. Qa3 { [%clk 0:03:27] } e5 { [%clk 0:03:51] To free up the LS bishop. Another way of doing this would be Bd7 --> e1 --> etc, but Aman decides on this way instead, a bit more aggressively since we are up a piece. } 14. dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:23] } Bxg4 { [%clk 0:03:47] } 15. Qc3 { [%clk 0:02:56] This simply gives up the exchange. } Bxd1 { [%clk 0:03:30] } 16. Kxd1 { [%clk 0:02:55] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:03:24] } 17. Qe1 { [%clk 0:02:53] Attacking the rook. } Raf8 { [%clk 0:03:14] Defending the rook, and bringing in more heavy attacking firepower at the same time. } (17... Rxb2 18. Qc3 Qxe5 19. Qxe5 Nxe5 { this line was also available. }) 18. e6 { [%clk 0:02:44] } Nxc5 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 19. e7 { [%clk 0:02:42] } Qxe7 { [%clk 0:03:08] } 20. Qb4 { [%clk 0:02:39] Pinning the knight. } Rxf1+ { [%clk 0:03:00] However, White's last move also undefended his bishop, and so White is another piece down. } 21. Rxf1 { [%clk 0:02:37] } Rxf1+ { [%clk 0:03:00] } 22. Ke2 { [%clk 0:02:33] } Qf8 { [%clk 0:02:38] A nice efficient move, because it both defends the rook and also frees the knight from the pin. And also threatens mate in one!
Aman pointed out the flashier move Rf2+. If Kxf2 then Nd3+ wins the queen! } (22... Rf2+ 23. Kxf2 Nd3+ 24. Kg2 Qxb4 { this was available, but Aman went for "Canadian efficiency" }) 23. Qa3 { [%clk 0:02:15] } Qf2# { [%clk 0:02:35] 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } 0-1

