{ youtu.be/VAHDgpJTJ4w?si=1Qw3ui3JgQ1kQ3AR&t=22096 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. c4 { [%clk 0:04:59] Our first 'pure' Queen's Gambit. } e6 { [%clk 0:04:50] Not c6, which may well end up opening the c-file.
In the Stonewall, it's the e-file that we want to open. } 3. cxd5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:50] QGD accepted variation. } 4. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:45] We go straight in with the Stonewall plan.
This move clamps down on the e4 square (i.e. preventing the move e4 by White). } (4... Nf6 5. Bg5 c6 6. e3 Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Nge2 (8. Nf3 Re8 9. O-O Bg4 10. h3 Bh5 11. g4 Bg6 12. Nh4 Bxd3 13. Qxd3 h6 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 { A conservative French exchange may have played out something like this. }) 8... Re8 9. h3 Nbd7 { A conservative French exchange set up may have gone something like this. }) 5. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:43] Keeping the knight out of b5 and adding a defender to the d pawn. } 6. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 7. e3 { [%clk 0:04:49] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:35] Simple Stonewall moves. Nothing special so far. } 8. Bg3 { [%clk 0:04:47] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:33] } 9. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:45] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:28] Here we go ... } 10. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:31] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 11. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:26] How should Black proceed from here? Some considerations:
(1) We'd like to play Nd7 at some point (followed by Nf6), developing the N.
(2) We could play Bxg3, but that may prove counter-productive, since hxg3 opens up the h-file for his rook. } Qe7 { [%clk 0:03:49] Black couldn't have played Nd7 yet, because that would drop his bishop.
So this patient move makes it possible next turn. } (11... Nd7?? 12. Bxd6 { would blunder the bishop }) (11... Qc7 12. Bxd6 Qxd6 { Qc7 also an option. Often played when the opponent is threatening a knight on e5 and we want extra defense on that square. There is no such threat here so Qf7 achieves the same thing (allows for Nd7) but is more flexible, as out queen is generally happier on the king side where we are looking to generate an attack. }) 12. Qc2 { [%clk 0:04:07] Possibly indicating that White is planning to 0-0-0. } Bg4 { [%clk 0:03:25] So Aman puts a stop to that straight away. } 13. f3?? { [%clk 0:04:01] Aman was hoping that White would respond with this move, because it 'loosens up' (i.e. undefends in this case) the e3 pawn. } exf3 { [%clk 0:03:20] } 14. gxf3 { [%clk 0:04:00] } Qxe3+ { [%clk 0:03:16] } 15. Ne2 { [%clk 0:03:51] } (15. Kf1 Bh3# { good way to lose. }) 15... Bf5 { [%clk 0:01:47] This move hits the queen with tempo, and enables this bishop to come in from a different angle.
(Note: again, we don't want to play Bxg3 because this would open up the h-file for White)
Other possible moves Aman was considering here:
(1) Bxf3 Nxf3 Qxf3 (i.e. winning a pawn).
(2) Re8 - adding more pressure down the e-file (in fact, threatening mate in one). } (15... Bxg3+ 16. hxg3 Nd7 17. Qxh7+ Kf7 18. fxg4 { this is a threat on Bxg3. Winning for white. }) (15... Bxf3 16. Nxf3 Qxf3 17. O-O-O { Aman considered this before deciding on Bf5. }) (15... Re8 16. O-O-O Bxf3 17. Bxd6 Bxh1 18. Rxh1 Qxe2 { this was also possible. }) 16. Qc1?? { [%clk 0:03:12] Now White can't castle. opponent now unable to castle. } Bd3 { [%clk 0:01:39] Aman continues with his plan for this bishop. He's now threatening to win the pinned knight. } 17. Qd1 { [%clk 0:02:57] } Re8 { [%clk 0:01:28] The pressure down the e-file is unbearable.
Note how Aman's Bd6 is loose. adding pressure. } 18. Bf2 { [%clk 0:02:46] This move doesn't save the knight.
Note: if White had played Bxd6, Aman would have played Bxe2. } Qxe2+ { [%clk 0:01:13] } 19. Qxe2 { [%clk 0:02:43] } Rxe2+ { [%clk 0:01:13] This is a fork, winning either the B or N. } 20. Kf1 { [%clk 0:02:31] } Rxd2+ { [%clk 0:01:10] } 21. Ke1 { [%clk 0:02:27] } Re2+ { [%clk 0:01:07] } 22. Kd1? { [%clk 0:02:25] } (22. Kf1 { this was needed. }) 22... Rxf2 { [%clk 0:01:05] ... or both in this case!
Note how Aman's attack has been possible without even touching his Nb8 or Ra8. } 23. Rg1 { [%clk 0:02:24] } Bf4 { [%clk 0:01:02] Aman has his sights on checkmate now .... } 24. Ke1 { [%clk 0:02:21] } Be3 { [%clk 0:00:55] Defends the rook.
Also note how these two imperious bishops act as a kind of invisible force-field around the king, who can't step onto the 2nd rank (even if the Rf2 wasn't there), as well as restricting him to two squares on the 1st rank. } 25. Rg5 { [%clk 0:02:16] This undefends the upcoming checkmate ... } Rf1# { [%clk 0:00:50] 0-1 Black wins by checkmate. } 0-1

