{ https://youtu.be/DYjp2MrfxkA?si=tblwXXEAUXINE4gK&t=989 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] Is this a stonewall brah?? } e6 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:51] Stopping e4 } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:47] Completing the pawn chain. With black, we often complete the pawn chain, like this, before moving any pieces out. This is an important move to keep the knight out of b5. That c3 knight of our opponent's is really bad now. We can afford the tempi because the position is closed. } 5. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 6. O-O { [%clk 0:04:49] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:45] Stopping Ne5. Later on in the series, Aman will put less emphasis on stopping the Ne5 move as black, and go for plans like the LSB maneuver more. I need to check if this is due to higher elos needing more complex plans or else if there are features of the position that call for one over another. Here both white knights are on their standard squares with the bishop exerting no real pressure and the DSB locked in. } (6... Nf6 { Later on in the speed run, Aman tends to play Nf6 faster. Possibly that is in French exchange positions with more urgency to castle. I will need to review the games again and check. } 7. Ne5 O-O { If our opponent plays Ne5 we just accept it and castle. } 8. b3 Nbd7 9. f4 Ne4 10. Nxe4 fxe4 11. Be2 Nxe5 12. dxe5 Bc5 13. a4 a5 14. Qd2 g5 15. fxg5 Rxf1+ 16. Bxf1 Qxg5 { And here we might try a g pawn break to generate some king side play etc. }) 7. Ne2 { [%clk 0:04:31] } Ngf6 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 8. Ng3 { [%clk 0:04:28] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:38] } 9. b3 { [%clk 0:04:15] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 10. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:03] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 11. Ne5?? { [%clk 0:04:00] our Nd7 prophylactic move paid off. You can't go their, buddy. } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 12. dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:57] } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:04:32] picking up a central pawn. } 13. Rb1 { [%clk 0:03:52] } Qh4 { [%clk 0:04:22] } 14. c4 { [%clk 0:03:10] } (14. Qh5 Bxg3 15. Qxh4 Bxh4 { On Qh5 Aman had this trick in mind. }) 14... Rf6 { [%clk 0:04:14] rook lift } 15. cxd5?? { [%clk 0:03:05] major blunder. Our "bad bishop" is about to become a MONSTER. } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:12] } (15... Rh6 16. h3 exd5 17. Bb2 Bxh3 18. gxh3 Qxh3 19. Qxd5+ cxd5 20. Rfc1 Bxg3 21. fxg3 Qh1+ 22. Kf2 Rh2# { The engine wanted this line. }) 16. Bb2 { [%clk 0:02:47] } Rh6 { [%clk 0:04:09] ignoring the threat on the DSB. Were we're going we're not going to need it. } 17. Bxe5?? { [%clk 0:02:45] blunders mate in one. } (17. h3 Bxh3 18. Bxe5 Bxg2 19. f3 Qh2+ 20. Kf2 Bxf3+ 21. Ke1 Bxd1 22. Rxd1 Rg6 23. Rd2 Qh4 24. Rh1 Qg5 25. Bf4 Qf6 26. Rh3 Rf8 27. Rdh2 Qc3+ 28. Ke2 Qb2+ 29. Kf1 Qc1+ 30. Ke2 Qc2+ 31. Ke1 Qc1+ 32. Ke2 d4 33. Nxe4 Qc2+ 34. Kf3 Qd1+ 35. Re2 d3 36. Nc3 dxe2 37. Nxd1 exd1=Q+ 38. Kf2 Rg1 39. Rg3 Qf1# { Even with a more resourceful and spirited defense, our opponent was dead lost. }) 17... Qxh2# { [%clk 0:04:04] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

