{ https://youtu.be/N2T3qPMCtBU?si=2hEw-Yfr5X7anO-Z&t=971 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. e3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:51] Again stopping Ne5 not a priority. We set up our stonewall pawn structure first. } 5. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:55] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 6. O-O { [%clk 0:04:54] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:40] Allowing Ne5. With that LSB eyeing our f pawn we need to keep our LSB eyeing the pawn should c4 happen. } (6... Nd7 { Aman could have taken the time to stop Ne5 here but doesn't. In the post game analysis, he explains he was reluctant to play this because of c4. } 7. c4 Ngf6 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bxf5 { Now if we take back how we'd like, with the e pawn, we would drop the f pawn. }) 7. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:42] Allowing Ne5 again. We live with this as black. Aman explains in the post game analysis that there just isn't a good way to stop it if the opponent is determined enough. } (7. c4 Nbd7 8. c5 Bc7 9. b4 O-O 10. Bb2 Ne4 11. Ne5 Nxe5 12. dxe5 Bxe5 13. Bxe5 { If our opponent didn't play Ne5 on Nf6 we would have played Nd7 to try to stop it, but even then, a determined opponent can bring the bishop to bear on the e5 square and force it eventually. }) 7... O-O { [%clk 0:04:37] } 8. f3 { [%clk 0:04:40] stopping Ne4 } Nbd7 { [%clk 0:04:27] No pressure from the bishop on our DSB so we can play the immediate Nd7. } 9. f4 { [%clk 0:04:35] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:20] } 10. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:21] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:16] } 11. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:19] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:03:41] Here Aman elects for imbalances rather than taking the knight. He has ideas of trying to get the LSB to a6 or h5 to trade it off. } (11... Nxe5 12. fxe5 { A symmetrical, boring position. }) 12. b3 { [%clk 0:04:06] } Qe7 { [%clk 0:03:20] Not sure why Aman moves the queen here. } 13. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:04] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 14. Be1 { [%clk 0:04:02] } Be8 { [%clk 0:03:04] } 15. Bh4 { [%clk 0:04:01] Playing the DSB maneuver (as white) against us. This guy is 700 elo? } c5 { [%clk 0:02:54] c pawn break to loosen the e5 knight. Also opens up the diagonal for the LSB and a possible avenue to trade LSBs off the board. } 16. c3 { [%clk 0:03:53] } Rc8 { [%clk 0:02:45] Bringing the rook to the c file, which is likely to open up. } 17. c4 { [%clk 0:03:33] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:02:38] we take because if he recaptures exd4 it gives us a protected passed pawn on e4. In these grindy symmetrical SW games this is often the best we can hope for in terms of favorable imbalances. } 18. exd4 { [%clk 0:03:29] } Kh8 { [%clk 0:02:14] "going to move my king out of the way here" doesn't really explain why. Perhaps something to do with the pawn tension likely to resolve soon and the g8-a2 diagonal becoming weak. } 19. Bg4 { [%clk 0:03:25] attacking the pawn and pinning it to the rook. } Rd8 { [%clk 0:01:58] White is better here according to the engine. So 700elo in chess.com is outplaying grandmasters? Interesting. Unpinning the rook. Also placing it inline with the queen. } 20. f5?? { [%clk 0:03:22] first bad move this guy's made. Loosens the knight on e5, which was a major asset for him, but will now be removed, and at the loss of a pawn to boot. } exf5 { [%clk 0:01:12] } (20... dxc4 21. bxc4 (21. Nxc4 { The opponent has this resource so Aman decided against dxc4. }) 21... Bxe5 22. dxe5 Rxd1 { Here the engine wanted tactics. }) 21. Bxf5? { [%clk 0:03:15] Compounding his error. } (21. Rxf5 { This was needed according to the engine. }) 21... Bxe5 { [%clk 0:01:01] } 22. dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:13] } Qxe5 { [%clk 0:00:59] Drops the pawn. } 23. Qe2 { [%clk 0:02:47] Another good move from our "700" } Bg6 { [%clk 0:00:41] } 24. Bxg6 { [%clk 0:02:43] } hxg6 { [%clk 0:00:40] } 25. Rad1 { [%clk 0:02:42] } d4 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 26. Bg3 { [%clk 0:02:29] } Qe6 { [%clk 0:00:33] } 27. Rf4 { [%clk 0:02:24] } g5 { [%clk 0:00:30] } 28. Rff1 { [%clk 0:02:09] } Rfe8 { [%clk 0:00:28] bringing in the firepower behind the connected passed pawns. } 29. Qb2 { [%clk 0:02:00] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:00:20] } 30. a4 { [%clk 0:01:47] } d3+ { [%clk 0:00:17] } 31. Kh1 { [%clk 0:01:42] And now we see the power of connected passed pawns. } e3 { [%clk 0:00:16] } 32. Rf3 { [%clk 0:01:34] } e2 { [%clk 0:00:15] } 33. Rg1 { [%clk 0:01:17] } d2 { [%clk 0:00:13] unstoppable. } 34. Qb1 { [%clk 0:00:59] } d1=Q { [%clk 0:00:11] } 35. Qc1 { [%clk 0:00:54] } Qbxg1# { [%clk 0:00:07] 0-1 Black wins. Strongest 700 I ever saw. Looking at his Chess.com profile now, he's 900 blitz with an all time high of 999. Maybe we just played into an opening he had studied in depth. } 0-1

