{ https://youtu.be/LrJH6AeNLCk?si=n91v6s33FSQ1POSj&t=2271 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 4. d4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:43] Prioritizing f5 and Nf6. We are in a race to get our king out of the open e file before our opponent can check us. } (4... Bd6 5. Bd3 (5. c4 Nf6 6. c5 Be7 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O b6 9. cxb6 axb6 10. Nc3 Bg4 11. h3 Bh5 12. g4 Bg6 13. Bxg6 hxg6 14. Bf4 Re8 { A possible continuation in the French exchange. }) 5... f5 6. O-O Nf6 7. Re1+ { Aman is concerned about getting checked in this open position. Hence the early f5. }) 5. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:47] } (5. c4 Bd6 6. Bg5 Nf6 7. Qe2+ Qe7 8. Bxf6 gxf6 9. Qxe7+ Bxe7 10. cxd5 { This can end up happening if we try to force the SW with black in the French exchange. c4 is a powerful move for white in these positions. }) 5... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 6. O-O { [%clk 0:04:43] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:23] } 7. Re1+ { [%clk 0:04:42] And we get checked anyway. } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:21] we could have played this in the alternative line playing Bd6 first. } 8. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:33] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:19] } 9. Nxe4?? { [%clk 0:04:30] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:18] } 10. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:24] } dxe4 { [%clk 0:04:17] } 11. Rxe4 { [%clk 0:04:23] } Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:14] } 12. Re2 { [%clk 0:04:13] } Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 13. Qd3 { [%clk 0:03:39] } Rxf3 { [%clk 0:03:18] Aman goes for fireworks. } (13... Bxf3 14. gxf3 Qh4 { the move conventional approach }) 14. gxf3 { [%clk 0:03:39] } Qh4 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 15. Qc4+ { [%clk 0:03:28] } Kf8 { [%clk 0:03:06] } (15... Kh8 16. Re8+ Bf8 17. Rxf8# { don't blunder mate! }) 16. f4 { [%clk 0:02:50] } (16. Re8+ Kxe8 17. Qg8+ Kd7 18. Qxg7+ Kc6 19. Qxg4 Qxh2+ 20. Kf1 { our opponent had these resources available. }) 16... Bh3 { [%clk 0:02:11] Has the benefit of keeping the king in a box/ mating net. } (16... Bf3 17. Re3 Qg4+ 18. Kf1 Qg2+ 19. Ke1 { Engine wanted Bf3. Aman was concerned tha