{ https://youtu.be/Rt93_cWZUUA?si=ldZpOz0VU3RCroIT&t=939 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:53] } (3... Bb4 { would have played this if following Aman's French speed run here. }) 4. exf5 { [%clk 0:04:46] } exf5 { [%clk 0:04:50] Forcing it. A terrible sequence of moves allowing Qh5+ } 5. Qe2+ { [%clk 0:04:32] } (5. Qh5+ g6 6. Qe2+ Be7 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bxe7 Nxe7 9. O-O-O O-O 10. Re1 Nbc6 { Might get this kind of position against a stronger player. Not so comfortable. }) 5... Be7 { [%clk 0:04:45] we go with the bishop because we want our knight on f6. } 6. Qe5 { [%clk 0:04:24] with ideas of Qxg7, trapping the rook. } (6. Qb5+ c6 { this check is easily met with c6 }) 6... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:39] Preventing Qxg7 and developing where we want the knight anyway. Also covers the d pawn. } 7. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:18] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:03:31] Another example of playing Nc6 as an exception. Hitting the queen. Our opponent's threat is that we are forced to recapture after Bxf6 with the g pawn because the DSB is pinned. Therefore this (hitting the queen--danger levels) is the best move according to the engine. Nd7 is our normal move and would have also hit the queen but it would have blundered the f pawn. } (7... Nbd7 8. Qxf5 { Blundering the pawn. Engine thinks it's still OK though. }) (7... O-O 8. Bxf6 Bxf6 9. Qxd5+ Qxd5 10. Nxd5 { Castling would have allowed a queen trade and for our opponent to win a pawn, with an active knight (albeit with compensation via an open file to the king). }) 8. Bb5 { [%clk 0:03:53] hard pinning the knight. These moves aren't the best but our opponent has succeeded in stopping us playing our normal Ne4. } Kf7 { [%clk 0:02:50] Aman didn't like castling because he is worried about dropping the d5 pawn. I believe he was miscalculating and the best move was indeed O-O here which is explained in an alternate line.
Later on in the video, Aman revealed the dark arts were involved in this decision, as he expected this subtle breaking of the pin to possibly be missed by our opponent, who had a high chance of therefore losing their queen which was suddenly attacked by the knight on c6. however, our opponent was wise to it and took Bxc6. } (8... O-O 9. Bxf6 Nxe5 { It seems Aman miscalculated here, because after O-O our knight is no longer hard pinned so we can meet Nxf6 with Nxe5 winning our opponent's queen. So O-O was indeed the best move. }) 9. Bxc6 { [%clk 0:03:37] } bxc6 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 10. Kd1 { [%clk 0:03:27] mouseslip? Aman doesn't even comment on it. } Re8 { [%clk 0:02:03] } (10... Ne4 11. Bxe7 Qxe7 12. Qxe7+ Kxe7 13. Nxe4 fxe4 { Ne4 leads to a big trade which Aman wanted to avoid }) 11. Bxf6 { [%clk 0:03:01] } Bxf6 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 12. Qg3 { [%clk 0:02:55] } Rb8 { [%clk 0:01:48] we choose to bring our rook to an open file, to take advantage over dark squared weaknesses. } (12... Bxd4 { The engine wanted this and Aman did see it but he chose to prioritize piece activity on the open file. }) 13. b3 { [%clk 0:02:53] weakening the dark squares and only we have a DSB. } Bxd4 { [%clk 0:01:17] forces the knight to e2 to defend, which is better for us than the knight going to f3 then threatening Ne5. } (13... Qe7 14. Nf3 { Qe7 threatens mate but is easily met with Nf3 }) 14. Nge2 { [%clk 0:02:49] } Be5 { [%clk 0:01:09] } 15. Qf3 { [%clk 0:02:38] } Qf6 { [%clk 0:01:04] building up the pressure on the knight. } 16. Qh5+ { [%clk 0:02:23] } Kf8 { [%clk 0:00:49] } 17. Qf3 { [%clk 0:02:06] } Ba6 { [%clk 0:00:42] } 18. Re1 { [%clk 0:01:42] } Bxe2+ { [%clk 0:00:23] removing a defender of the knight. Nxe2 would drop the rook. } 19. Rxe2 { [%clk 0:01:39] } Bxc3 { [%clk 0:00:21] cashing on on the weakness we invoked way back on move 12. Rb8. } 20. Rxe8+ { [%clk 0:01:35] forced mate from here. } Rxe8 { [%clk 0:00:21] } 21. Qxc3 { [%clk 0:01:08] } Re1+ { [%clk 0:00:13] Aman went with this line to demonstrate how even low on time and premoving you could win this game. } (21... Qxc3 22. a3 Re1# { Aman had mate in 2 but decided to play the alternative line for educational purposes. }) 22. Qxe1 { [%clk 0:01:05] } Qxa1+ { [%clk 0:00:13] } 23. Ke2 { [%clk 0:01:04] } Qxe1+ { [%clk 0:00:13] All premoves. } 24. Kxe1 { [%clk 0:01:03] } c5 { [%clk 0:00:12] } 25. Ke2 { [%clk 0:01:01] } Ke7 { [%clk 0:00:12] } 26. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:59] } Kd6 { [%clk 0:00:12] } 27. Kf4 { [%clk 0:00:58] } Ke6 { [%clk 0:00:11] } 28. h3 { [%clk 0:00:55] } Kf6 { [%clk 0:00:10] } 29. g4 { [%clk 0:00:54] } g5+ { [%clk 0:00:09] } 30. Kf3 { [%clk 0:00:51] } Ke5 { [%clk 0:00:09] } 31. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:49] } fxg4 { [%clk 0:00:07] } 32. hxg4 { [%clk 0:00:48] } d4+ { [%clk 0:00:06] } 33. Kf3 { [%clk 0:00:42] } Kd5 { [%clk 0:00:06] } 34. a3 { [%clk 0:00:35] } h6 { [%clk 0:00:06] } 35. a4 { [%clk 0:00:33] } c4 { [%clk 0:00:06] } 36. bxc4+ { [%clk 0:00:27] } Kxc4 { [%clk 0:00:06] } 37. Ke4 { [%clk 0:00:26] } c5 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 38. f3 { [%clk 0:00:22] } Kc3 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 39. f4 { [%clk 0:00:17] } gxf4 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 40. Kxf4 { [%clk 0:00:15] } c4 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 41. g5 { [%clk 0:00:09] } hxg5+ { [%clk 0:00:04] } 42. Kxg5 { [%clk 0:00:07] } Kxc2 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 43. Kf4 { [%clk 0:00:07] } d3 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 44. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:06] } c3 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 45. Kd4 { [%clk 0:00:04] } d2 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 46. Kc5 { [%clk 0:00:03] } d1=Q { [%clk 0:00:03] } 47. Kc6 { [%clk 0:00:02] } Qa1 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 48. Kb7 { [%clk 0:00:01] } Kd3 { [%clk 0:00:03] 0-1 Black wins on time. a masterclass in premoving. } 0-1

