... Qa3# ideas to follow. } 17. Rhg1+ { [%clk 0:03:26] } Kh8 { [%clk 0:02:39] } 18. Qe5+ { [%clk 0:03:22] stopping our mating ideas but a queen trade while up material is nearly always welcome. } Qxe5 { [%clk 0:02:35] } 19. dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:20] } Be6 { [%clk 0:02:27] presumably to blockade the passed pawn, defend the weak f and d pawns, and connect the rooks. } 20. f4 { [%clk 0:03:19] } Rg8 { [%clk 0:02:24] Offering more trades. } 21. Rg5 { [%clk 0:03:13] } h6 { [%clk 0:02:23] intermezzo. } (21... Rxg5?! 22. hxg5 { taking immediately would have been an inaccuracy as it would have allowed him to connect his pawns. }) 22. Rg3 { [%clk 0:02:54] good move by the opponent. He wants to improve his pawn structure. } Nc4 { [%clk 0:02:17] } 23. Rdg1 { [%clk 0:02:49] } Kh7 { [%clk 0:02:13] } 24. a4 { [%clk 0:02:38] } a5 { [%clk 0:02:02] with b5 ideas, looking to convert the queenside pawn majority into a passed pawn. } 25. Kb1 { [%clk 0:02:09] } Nd2+ { [%clk 0:01:55] } 26. Kb2 { [%clk 0:02:06] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:01:55] centralizing the knight. } 27. R3g2 { [%clk 0:01:59] } Rxg2 { [%clk 0:01:45] now we can trade without fixing our opponent's pawns. } 28. Rxg2 { [%clk 0:01:57] } Rg8 { [%clk 0:01:45] } 29. Re2 { [%clk 0:01:50] } Rg1 { [%clk 0:01:38] } 30. Kb3 { [%clk 0:01:41] } Rb1+ { [%clk 0:01:29] } 31. Ka2 { [%clk 0:01:39] } Nxc3+ { [%clk 0:01:26] } 32. Ka3 { [%clk 0:01:35] mate in 5. Aman asks the "chat GMs" to find it. } b5 { [%clk 0:00:47] } 33. axb5 { [%clk 0:01:12] } cxb5 { [%clk 0:00:04] gangster stuff from the dark magician as he deliberately allows the time to run down to 4s so that our opponent will spot Bxf5. In our opponent's head, Aman premoves the entire sequence of moves to follow leading to mate. Proving once and for all chess is game of psychological warfare. Only the truly sadistic can make it. } 34. Bxf5+ { [%clk 0:01:09] } Bxf5 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 35. Re4 { [%clk 0:01:07] } b4+ { [%clk 0:00:04] } 36. Rxb4 { [%clk 0:01:02] } axb4# { [%clk 0:00:04] 0-1 Black wins. savage. } 0-1

