1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:55] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 4. e3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:49] Aman doesn't explain why he sets up the pawns before bringing out pieces with black. With white he seems to love an early Bd3 but with black he often sets up the pawns first and even then often plays Nf6 before playing Bd6.
He wants to play Bd6 next move and "we don't mind the trade" (recapture with Qxd6).
What we DO want to avoid is trading for the opponents DSB which would allow them to take back with exf4 and play f3 and then they control e4 and e5 and that's not something we can allow. } 5. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:38] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 6. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:31] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 7. Bxf6 { [%clk 0:04:29] Aman thought this capture was hasty. } Qxf6 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 8. f4 { [%clk 0:04:24] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:39] We castle here. The opponents position is copying ours but is a slightly worse version. With that said, the knight is hard to remove. Nd7->Nxe5 ideas don't work because he forks our queen and bishop with the recapture. Aman is thinking about moves like c5, g5 to try and remove those strong central pawn defenders of the knight. If we can take one of those pawns, then place our knight on e4, then he takes, we can recapture and create a passed pawn. But only if he can get the opponents e pawn to move. } 9. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:48] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:25] Aman thought about g5 but elects to develop the bishop first. The plan is to re-route it via e8 and then play Nd7. Aman considers Nxd7 as silly as the opponent would be trading a monster knight for a weak and passive bishop. } 10. Qe2 { [%clk 0:03:31] "Qe7 is a strange move." He wants to castle but nevertheless Aman thinks something is off with the move. } Be8 { [%clk 0:04:23] } 11. O-O-O { [%clk 0:03:22] In the post game analysis Aman says that 0-0-0 by the opponent makes g5 is not that interesting anymore. } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 12. Ndf3 { [%clk 0:03:21] "He's ready to take the knight back [with a knight]". } Qh6 { [%clk 0:03:39] Qh6 is played because Aman wants to avoid potential fork issues with recapture of the knight on e5 should he take it, but also because he wants to play Bh5 and says he can't do it without the preparatory queen move. } 13. Nxd7 { [%clk 0:03:03] Nice move by the opponent. Prevents both Bh5 and Nf6. } Bxd7 { [%clk 0:03:39] } 14. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:02] } Be8 { [%clk 0:03:37] still intending Bh5. } 15. h4?? { [%clk 0:02:54] In the post game analysis Aman says that this was a huge blunder. } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:22] Aman takes the knight because he has a follow up with a winning tactic Bh5. If he did not remove the knight then g4 would be possible as a response. } 16. dxe5 { [%clk 0:02:51] } Bh5 { [%clk 0:03:21] } 17. Qd2 { [%clk 0:02:40] } Bxd1 { [%clk 0:03:21] } 18. Qxd1 { [%clk 0:02:40] } Rad8 { [%clk 0:03:14] to support a d4 push. A capture by the e pawn would leave the f pawn hanging. } 19. g4 { [%clk 0:02:34] "super aggressive move". } fxg4 { [%clk 0:03:11] } 20. Qxg4 { [%clk 0:02:33] } d4 { [%clk 0:03:08] If the opponent plays Bc4, Aman would play dxe3, hanging the e6 pawn but Aman thinks tactically it should be good to "shake up the position". He is up material so is focussed on opening up the position. } 21. Bc4 { [%clk 0:02:10] } dxe3 { [%clk 0:03:07] } 22. Bxe6+ { [%clk 0:02:00] } Kh8 { [%clk 0:03:06] } 23. Qg5 { [%clk 0:01:25] "a pretty good move" } Qxe6 { [%clk 0:01:56] Aman considered some potential sacs for mate here but couldn't find anything concrete and went for the solid option to capture the material. } 24. Rg1 { [%clk 0:01:06] } Qd7 { [%clk 0:01:46] defending mate while setting up a tactic Qd2, Qe1! } 25. e6 { [%clk 0:01:01] } Qd2+ { [%clk 0:01:40] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

