{ https://youtu.be/yUFSsm7s3-E?si=_b-1k7B6s5Ku0t3j&t=247 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:53] } (4... Bd6 5. Be2 Nc6 6. Nc3 Nge7 7. Nb5 Bf5 8. Nxd6+ Qxd6 9. O-O O-O-O 10. Re1 f6 11. Nh4 Bd7 12. Nf3 g5 13. a4 h5 14. c3 h4 15. b4 g4 16. Nd2 g3 17. fxg3 hxg3 18. h3 Nf5 19. Nb3 Nh4 20. a5 Nxg2 21. Kxg2 Rxh3 22. Rh1 Rdh8 23. Rxh3 Rxh3 24. Bf1 Ne7 25. a6 b6 26. Nc5 Nf5 27. Nxd7 Qxd7 28. Bf4 Qh7 29. Qf3 Rh2+ 30. Kg1 Rf2 31. Qh1 Qxh1+ 32. Kxh1 Rh2+ 33. Kg1 Nh4 34. Bh3+ Rxh3 35. Re1 g2 36. Re8+ Kd7 37. Rg8 Rh1+ 38. Kf2 Rf1+ 39. Ke3 g1=Q+ 40. Rxg1 Rxg1 41. Ke2 Rg2+ 42. Kf1 Ra2 43. Bh6 { How a French exchange aggressive variation may have played out. }) 5. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } (5. c4 Nf6 6. Bd3 c6 7. O-O Bd6 8. Re1+ Ne4 9. Bg5 Qd7 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. Qb3 O-O 12. Qxd5+ Kh8 13. Bxe4 fxe4 14. Qxe4 { The Stonewall from the exchange French can always be steamrolled by an opponent playing the best, engine approved play. Aman's opponents rarely (pretty much never) play these lines, though. And even though the engine does quite often evaluate his opening as shakey, he has the mouse / middle game / end game skills to make it work. }) 5... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 6. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 7. g3 { [%clk 0:04:49] "doesn't look like a good move". } O-O { [%clk 0:04:39] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:48] } Qe8 { [%clk 0:04:38] "let's go for the classic Qe8". } 9. Re1 { [%clk 0:04:46] Sarcastically "oh no! rook e8!" } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:36] Bam. Hitting 'em with the main idea. } 10. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:40] } Qg6 { [%clk 0:04:14] Aman considered Qh5 and Qg6 here. He didn't explain why he chose Qg6. He drew lines on the board suggesting if Bf5, Bxf5. Not sure why we'd do this trade now since to-date we've been avoiding it. Must be because his g pawn is pinned in that scenario so he would not be able to recapture. } 11. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:30] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:03:49] Aman considered f5 but "it's not the right vibe" since we want to keep the stonewall knight anchored there as a thorn in our opponent's side. } 12. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:20] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:03:48] } 13. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:20] } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:31] We would normally recapture with the knight but Aman elects to give up the DSB with the aim of taking advantage of the opponent's weak light squares in front of the king. } 14. dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:20] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:31] } 15. f4 { [%clk 0:04:19] This move surprised Aman. } Bg4 { [%clk 0:03:00] Aman did not play en passant because he didn't see a continuation leading to mate. The bishop hits the queen so our opponent can't capture the knight. } 16. Qc1 { [%clk 0:04:11] } Nf3+ { [%clk 0:02:59] } 17. Nxf3 { [%clk 0:04:10] } Bxf3 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 18. Kf2 { [%clk 0:04:08] } Rad8 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 19. Qd2 { [%clk 0:04:07] } d4 { [%clk 0:02:48] sacking this pawn to bait Bxd4. The Queen is skewered by the rook so the Bishop is then pinned and falls to c5. } 20. Bxd4 { [%clk 0:04:04] } c5 { [%clk 0:02:47] } 21. c3 { [%clk 0:03:59] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:02:45] } 22. cxd4 { [%clk 0:03:59] } h5 { [%clk 0:02:43] } 23. h4 { [%clk 0:03:57] Aman now plans to gang up on the weak isolated d pawn to win the game. } Kh7 { [%clk 0:02:33] king safety. get off the weak diagonal. } 24. Qe3 { [%clk 0:03:55] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:02:29] hitting both d and b pawns. } 25. Rad1 { [%clk 0:03:46] blunders the exchange under pressure. } Bxd1 { [%clk 0:02:26] } 26. Rxd1 { [%clk 0:03:45] } Qxb2+ { [%clk 0:02:18] } 27. Rd2 { [%clk 0:03:45] } Qb1 { [%clk 0:02:17] } 28. Re2 { [%clk 0:03:42] } Qd3 { [%clk 0:02:12] } 29. Qxe4+ { [%clk 0:03:39] } Qxe4 { [%clk 0:02:11] } 30. Rxe4 { [%clk 0:03:39] } Rfe8 { [%clk 0:02:11] } 31. Ke3 { [%clk 0:03:38] } Kg6 { [%clk 0:02:08] } 32. Re5 { [%clk 0:03:36] } Rxe5+ { [%clk 0:02:07] } 33. dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:33] } Kf5 { [%clk 0:02:06] 0-1 Black wins. Once again the stonewall knight proves decisive. In the post game analysis Aman talks about how normally you could never jump a knight to block a check because you'd be walking into a pin and f4 would be coming. But the fact that the opponent can't attack the knight with a pawn and that we have two pawns anchoring it makes it such a powerful move. The opponent should have taken the knight with Bxf6 once we played Qe8 but people usually miss this probably for the reason outlined above--usually Re1 is a good move but not in this case. } 0-1

