{ https://youtu.be/yUFSsm7s3-E?si=I8z4j8LStJFU8O4E&t=1011 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3. f3 { [%clk 0:04:54] "f3, what the hell is that?!". } f5 { [%clk 0:04:48] } (3... d4 4. Nb5 a6 5. Na3 e5 { Best move is to push the pawns like this according to the engine. Like the French but we have the space. } 6. d3 f5 7. exf5 Bxf5) 4. a3 { [%clk 0:04:43] (sarcastically) "a3, ever a dangerous move" } c6 { [%clk 0:04:41] Once again we've seen Aman place the pawns before moving any pieces playing the stonewall as black. } 5. d3 { [%clk 0:04:42] "e3?!?! remarkable moves by the opponent here". (presumably sarcastic). } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 6. g3 { [%clk 0:04:38] At this point Aman's facial expression suggests he suspects the opponent is trolling } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:17] } 7. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:35] } h6 { [%clk 0:04:14] Aman removes recapture of the bishop by the queen in case of Bxf6 } 8. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:30] "very interesting opponent we've got here". Actually it is because what is Aman going to do with the knight considering our opponent has that jagged sort-of "reverse stonewall" keeping out the Ne4 idea. } O-O { [%clk 0:04:09] } 9. Qc1 { [%clk 0:04:26] (sarcastically) oh no! he's gonna checkmate me(!) } Nbd7 { [%clk 0:04:01] "When your opponent is up to something, just let him do it". } 10. f4 { [%clk 0:04:23] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:03:52] } 11. dxe4 { [%clk 0:04:20] } dxe4 { [%clk 0:03:49] } 12. Bc4 { [%clk 0:04:13] } Nb6 { [%clk 0:03:46] stonewall plans be dammed, our unorthodox opponent has taken us to paths unchartered. So Aman elects to "attack the bishop". Where we go from here is anybody's guess. } 13. Bb3 { [%clk 0:04:10] } Nbd5 { [%clk 0:03:41] Knight is blocking a good diagonal for our opponent. } 14. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:05] Aman remarks that the opponents queen is in the way of castling. I'm not sure this opponent is much of a castler, though. } Ng4 { [%clk 0:03:28] Be3 is screaming for Ng4. } 15. Bxd5 { [%clk 0:03:55] Aman was commenting that "there is no way the opponent can play Bxd5" prior to this move. It allows us to recapture and set up the perfect pawn structure. } exd5 { [%clk 0:03:26] beautiful pawn chain pointing ominously to the opponent's kingside. } 16. h3 { [%clk 0:03:51] } Nxe3 { [%clk 0:03:21] } 17. Qxe3 { [%clk 0:03:49] } Qe7 { [%clk 0:02:22] } 18. O-O-O { [%clk 0:03:44] } Bc5 { [%clk 0:02:20] } 19. Qe2 { [%clk 0:03:31] The opponent has taken the square his knight likely wanted to we are happy to see this move. } Be6 { [%clk 0:02:03] Bf5 he could play g4, f5 in response so Aman discounts it and plays this instead. No if he plays g4 we can take the f pawn. Aman is looking at Bxa3 ideas as a future plan. He wants to bring rooks to the middle before he does something like that. } 20. Qg2 { [%clk 0:03:06] } Bxa3 { [%clk 0:01:46] Aman elects to play the bishop sac now the queen has moved as he thinks he can make it work, force the king out, and at least win the piece back. } 21. bxa3 { [%clk 0:03:02] } Qxa3+ { [%clk 0:01:46] } 22. Kd2 { [%clk 0:03:00] } e3+ { [%clk 0:01:44] } 23. Kd3 { [%clk 0:02:55] } Bf5+ { [%clk 0:01:41] } 24. Kd4 { [%clk 0:02:51] 0-1 Black wins. He shows valiant commitment to protecting his knight but we can still win it with c5+, Kxd5, Qxc3, but Aman is also now thinking more about keeping the king exposed and preventing an escape over to the king side. Aman was thinking Rfe8 leading to a mate prior to the opponent resigning. } 0-1

