{ https://youtu.be/NJQGPORLNA0?si=CtGMOpfSxZz8JNJG&t=2924 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:56] In the previous game he blocked with f6, now with Be7. previous game similar but with c pawns pushed. } 4. Bxe7 { [%clk 0:04:57] } Qxe7 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 6. e3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 7. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:48] Aman considered Nf6 but played this. Maybe something to do with the DSB being off the board? White knight on f3 blocking the queen check on h5? } 8. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:53] } Qd6 { [%clk 0:04:43] Spending the extra time to play Qd6 and stop white's Ne5 ideas. } 9. Qc2 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Ngf6 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 10. h4 { [%clk 0:04:48] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:37] Considers castling but then plays an early Ne4. I think the first time we've seen it before castling, in white or black. Not sure why. (NB: after further analysis I see this is incorrect. We often see Ne5 before castling with white in these cases: 1: Punishing an opponent's time wasting in the opening 2: Blocking the bishop's attack on the f4 pawn in order that we can answer cxd4 with exd4. 3: Targeting our opponent's bishop when it is on the g6 square 4: When we are concerned our opponent might be playing f5 (with a knight on g4). With black we often see an early Ne4 when we are checked by a rook early in the opening, in the French exchange version of our Stonewall as black. )
In the post game review he said that castling was probably the best move here, but that "Ne4 was not going to be a mistake". } 11. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:29] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:36] In the post game analysis Aman said that after you play the fxe4 recapture, you should be thinking about the e5 advance as a follow up right after. } 12. Ng5 { [%clk 0:04:28] "not threatening much". In the post game review he lamented that he should have played h6 and kicked the knight out right away. Because O-O allowed f4, although the opponent didn't find that move. } O-O { [%clk 0:04:20] Aman considers h6 but elects to castle here. He's not scared of Nxe4. One issue in the position is that f4 is strong because our e pawn is pinned and can't capture en passant. } 13. Nb3 { [%clk 0:04:18] } h6 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 14. Nh3 { [%clk 0:04:17] } e5 { [%clk 0:04:11] The e5 advance following the fxe4 recapture is a key idea in the stonewall. It opens up the LSB. If they take, it gets your knight in. } 15. dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:15] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:11] } 16. O-O-O { [%clk 0:04:14] } Nd3+ { [%clk 0:04:07] } 17. Kb1 { [%clk 0:04:11] } Bxh3 { [%clk 0:04:05] f2 hanging after the recapture. } 18. gxh3 { [%clk 0:04:08] } Nxf2 { [%clk 0:04:03] } 19. Nd4 { [%clk 0:03:54] rxf2 was also tempting but after rd2 we are only winning a pawn, while the Knight capture wins material. } Nxh1 { [%clk 0:03:50] "our pawn structure is crushing". "If I am going to take something, it should probably be the thing in the corner?". I suppose because it forces his pieces out to the side of the board when recapturing. } 20. Rxh1 { [%clk 0:03:53] } Qg3 { [%clk 0:03:50] On Qb3 we have Rf7 to defend. We are threatening the e pawn and Rf2, Raf8. } 21. Qe2 { [%clk 0:03:31] } Rf2 { [%clk 0:03:40] Aman considered Qh5 as counterpleas but decides that it's not a concrete threat. } 22. Qg4 { [%clk 0:03:17] } Qxg4 { [%clk 0:03:34] We could have won a pawn with Qxe3 but Aman explains that in situation like this (up material) he will elect to simply to a winning end game each time. } 23. hxg4 { [%clk 0:03:16] } Raf8 { [%clk 0:03:32] } 24. Nf5 { [%clk 0:03:15] } h5 { [%clk 0:03:27] On Ne7+, Kf7. gxh5 can't be played because it would drop the knight. If he moves the knight we want to play Rf1 to trade the rooks. } 25. Ne7+ { [%clk 0:03:06] } Kf7 { [%clk 0:03:21] Kh7 was also an option. On Nf5, g6. } 26. Nf5 { [%clk 0:02:52] } g6 { [%clk 0:03:14] hxg4 also looked good. } 27. Nd6+ { [%clk 0:02:43] } Ke7 { [%clk 0:03:13] } 28. Nxb7 { [%clk 0:02:41] } Rf1+ { [%clk 0:03:13] this guarantees to trade the rooks. "after we trade the rooks, the rest is easy." } 29. Rxf1 { [%clk 0:02:40] } Rxf1+ { [%clk 0:03:12] } 30. Kc2 { [%clk 0:02:39] } hxg4 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 31. Nc5 { [%clk 0:02:37] } g3 { [%clk 0:03:12] We have ideas to push the passed g pawn. } 32. Kd2 { [%clk 0:02:33] } g2 { [%clk 0:03:11] } 33. Ke2 { [%clk 0:02:33] } g1=Q { [%clk 0:03:11] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

