{ https://youtu.be/NJQGPORLNA0?si=fZpvZjj1YZrUcwYm&t=3287 } 1. c4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. cxd5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. d4 { [%clk 0:04:56] Aman considered c6 here but he said he couldn't really play it because more important was f5 to stop the opponent playing e4. } f5 { [%clk 0:04:51] } (4... c6 5. Bf4 Nf6 6. e3 Bd6 7. Bxd6 Qxd6 8. Bd3 O-O { Should have probably transposed into French exchange conservative variation. }) 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 6. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:53] } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:48] Again offering the trade with Be7. In this case f6 was not even an option. Aman pre-moved recapture with the queen. } 7. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:52] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 8. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:43] Always ask your opponent if they want to repeat. } 9. Bxe7 { [%clk 0:04:49] } Qxe7 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 10. e3 { [%clk 0:04:48] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 11. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:48] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:34] Again Ne4 prior to O-O. I don't think we ever see this with white, but perhaps black being a tempo down forces more directness/ aggression? (NB: after further analysis I see this is incorrect. We often see Ne5 before castling with white in these cases: 1: Punishing an opponent's time wasting in the opening 2: Blocking the bishop's attack on the f4 pawn in order that we can answer cxd4 with exd4. 3: Targeting our opponent's bishop when it is on the g6 square 4: When we are concerned our opponent might be playing f5 (with a knight on g4). With black we often see an early Ne4 when we are checked by a rook early in the opening, in the French exchange version of our Stonewall as black. )
In the post game analysis Aman explains that he was concerned about Ne5 and didn't have a good way to stop that as Nd7 would have dropped the f pawn.
So it seems we have three ways to defend that important f pawn: the LSB from c8, blocking the attack via the knight jumping into e4, which also opens the f file for the rook to come in defend when we finally castle. } 12. O-O { [%clk 0:04:41] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:31] To prevent Ne5. } 13. Re1 { [%clk 0:04:39] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:29] } 14. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:35] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 15. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:35] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 16. dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:33] } Qxe5 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 17. Rf1 { [%clk 0:04:31] } Qg5 { [%clk 0:04:22] Bg4 (attacking the queen) or Bh3 (using the pin) ideas } 18. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:26] } Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:11] Bh3 he could respond with f4. So we play Bg4 with Bf3 ideas which is much stronger. } 19. Qd2 { [%clk 0:04:25] } Bf3 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 20. g3 { [%clk 0:04:24] 0-1 Black wins. Forced mate as white has no answer to Qg4, Qh3, Qg2# according to Aman. } 0-1

