{ https://youtu.be/o5ICyFPmOPg?si=jxL9hygGKi38PSzV&t=851 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:05:00] Looks like we may be facing a fellow wonestaller. A "student of the game".
In the post game analysis, Aman said that since he knew the opponent was playing the stonewall, he probably should have played Bf5, e6, as it would have been nice to have gotten the LSB out of the pawn chain. } e6 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 3. f4 { [%clk 0:04:56] "my guy". } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:40] avoiding the symmetrical stonewall. Aiming right for that weak e4 square. } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:52] In the post game analysis, Aman looked at the opponents game to critique the opponent's stonewall. Interestingly, he remarked that the opponent probably made a mistake by setting up the pawn moves first. Aman said "normally I don't do that, I think you need some pieces in there as well". While true of white, actually with black Aman frequently has set up the pawns first in our speed run so far. Aman doesn't really address why this is. } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:36] And straight in there. In the post game analysis, Aman says that this knight on e4 was the dominating factor in black's win. Especially since our opponent allowed us to support it with the other knight and the fianchettod bishop. } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:48] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 6. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:46] } Ndf6 { [%clk 0:04:30] Aman does not explain this but presumably we want to replace a knight with a knight on Bxe4? } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:04:42] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:25] } 8. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:40] Aman considers fianchetto here or else "taking and going Ne4" by which I assume he means Nxd2, Ne4. } b6 { [%clk 0:04:07] } 9. Qc2 { [%clk 0:04:38] } Bb7 { [%clk 0:04:03] I guess this move/set up is supporting the e4 knight. } 10. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:35] "beautiful". } O-O { [%clk 0:03:59] And finally we castle. Interesting how long we left it. I'm pretty sure this never happens with the white pieces. } 11. Ndf3 { [%clk 0:04:33] } c5 { [%clk 0:03:44] Aman comments that while both players are enjoying knight outposts, our knight cannot be kicked out, while our opponents can. So long term perhaps we have plans to re-route the knight and play f3. But for now Aman strikes at the center with this move. } 12. Ng5 { [%clk 0:04:28] "interesting move" } h6 { [%clk 0:03:39] } 13. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:21] } Nxe4 { [%clk 0:03:36] No hesitation on this recapture. } 14. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:13] "solid play" } Qe8 { [%clk 0:03:23] With f3 ideas. } 15. Rf3 { [%clk 0:04:11] } f6 { [%clk 0:03:18] } 16. Ng4 { [%clk 0:04:08] } Rc8 { [%clk 0:03:13] } 17. Raf1 { [%clk 0:03:58] } f5 { [%clk 0:02:53] finally we are playing the stonewall! } 18. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:55] } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:02:43] Aman considered cxd4 but thought it would be nicer to keep the bishop blocked by playing Bxe5. } 19. fxe5 { [%clk 0:03:53] } Bc6 { [%clk 0:02:41] with ideas/dreams of Bb5 to trade the LSBs. Aman would be quite happy with a BvN end game. As Silman says in Amateur Mind, if your opponent has the bishop pair, try trading one of the bishops off.
In the post game analysis, Aman described this as the worst case scenario for white playing the stonewall. Because, he can7t get rid of black's knight on e4 and because the LSB forced trade is coming and "the last thing you want is to trade LSBs in the stonewall" (as white). } 20. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:03:38] fxe4 would be a great way to lose the game, so we take dxe4 } dxe4 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 21. Rf4 { [%clk 0:03:36] } Bb5 { [%clk 0:02:31] anticipating c4 upon which we will play cxd4 because cxd5 is not possible due to the pin that would be "discovered" on the c pawn to the queen. Aman makes these speed runs look easy but when you break down things in detail you see how much his tactical awareness carries him through the middle game to say nothing of his GM end game skill and 3000+ elo bullet chess pre-moving skills / playing under time pressure skills. } 22. c4 { [%clk 0:03:29] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:02:29] } 23. Bb4 { [%clk 0:03:07] } Rxc4 { [%clk 0:01:45] Aman considers both Bxc4 and Rxc4. Bxc4 has the advantage that it makes an immediate threat of Bxf1. But Rxc4 is simpler and on Qd2, for example, we have Rxb4 winning more material after Qxb4 because we would have Bxf1 as a follow up. } 24. Qb3 { [%clk 0:03:03] } Rxb4 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 25. Qxb4 { [%clk 0:03:01] } Bxf1 { [%clk 0:01:41] } 26. Rxf1 { [%clk 0:02:57] } d3 { [%clk 0:01:40] Although dxe3 is a free pawn we push because we prefer the protected passed pawn. } 27. Rd1 { [%clk 0:02:49] } Qd7 { [%clk 0:01:36] 0-1 Black wins. Opponent resigns here but interestingly Aman had ideas of Rc8 as a follow up. I assume to infiltrate on the 7th rank or else d7 followed by Rc8 ideas? } 0-1

