{ https://youtu.be/o5ICyFPmOPg?si=3wygYs_7XBynipf-&t=2681 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4. d4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:51] Aman was considering both c6 and f5 here. He seemed torn 50/50 on which one to play. } (4... Bd6 5. Bd3 Ne7 6. O-O Nbc6 7. Bg5 f6 8. Bh4 Bg4 9. h3 Bh5 10. Nc3 Nxd4 { A better continuation would look something like this, the aggressive French exchange variation Aman teaches in his French opening speed run. }) 5. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:56] } (5. c4 Nf6 6. Bd3 c6 7. O-O Bd6 8. Re1+ Ne4 9. Bg5 Qd7 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. Nc3 O-O 12. Nxd5 { If our opponent knows what they're doing, playing moves like an immediate c4, then we can get into trouble fast trying to force the SW against e4. }) 5... Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 6. O-O { [%clk 0:04:55] And now an early Bd6 with black! Aman has been favoring Nf6 first so far with the black pieces. Perhaps due to no Qh5+? Also Bishop on e2 prevents a rook or queen check down the e file? Perhaps also a psychological aspect as Aman knows the opponent will likely castle next move. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 7. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:53] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:48] } 8. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:52] "a quick Ne5 from the lad" } Qe8 { [%clk 0:04:22] "I'm gonna go with the classic". Nd7 and h6 also fine moves in this position, according to Aman. } 9. f4 { [%clk 0:04:51] "I had a feeling he wanted to do this." } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 10. Bh4 { [%clk 0:04:44] } Be6 { [%clk 0:04:17] Aman didn't say why he played this bishop move, but "Bad bishops protect good pawns." RIP Danya.
Aman did say he definitely does not want to take the knight, as it will only give the opponent a protected passed pawn. } 11. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:27] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:14] } 12. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:18] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:10] } 13. b3 { [%clk 0:04:01] "Well guess what move he wants to play. No surprises here" Presumably c4? } c6 { [%clk 0:04:03] } 14. Rb1 { [%clk 0:03:56] "hang on... what the heck is Rb1?!" Preparing for the open b file maybe? But, buddy, we're not taking. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:03:07] Aman mentioned he would like to trade the LSBs by force. But it is currently tough. We play Nf6 for now. } 15. f5 { [%clk 0:03:28] f5 makes the e5 knight lose a lot of stability. So, generally speaking, we are happy to see it. } Bc8 { [%clk 0:02:47] Aman considered Bd7 but in that case the opponent could play Nxd7 so he elects to come all the way back. } 16. c4 { [%clk 0:03:26] } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:02:41] } 17. dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:20] } Qxe5 { [%clk 0:02:41] } 18. cxd5 { [%clk 0:02:53] } Nxd5 { [%clk 0:02:40] With Nc3 or Ne3 ideas. He has Bc4 but then the f pawn is falling. } 19. Bc4 { [%clk 0:02:38] } Bxf5 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 20. Bg3 { [%clk 0:02:29] } Qe6 { [%clk 0:02:27] Yes it's a nasty pin, but the knight is anchored to d5 via the supporting c pawn and can't be attacked by pawns so it is staying there as long as Aman wants so no real danger. } 21. Qd4 { [%clk 0:01:59] "a good looking move. I like it". } a6 { [%clk 0:02:15] For some reason plays this but with Rd8 ideas. A preparatory move to avoid Qxa7. } 22. Be5 { [%clk 0:01:51] } Rf7 { [%clk 0:02:07] Protecting the g pawn. Now the plan seems to have changed to trying for Re8. } 23. Rf4 { [%clk 0:01:36] } Nxf4 { [%clk 0:01:51] We have the fork follow up to effectively trade queens } 24. Bxe6 { [%clk 0:01:31] } Ne2+ { [%clk 0:01:50] } 25. Kf2 { [%clk 0:01:27] } Nxd4 { [%clk 0:01:50] } 26. Bxf7+ { [%clk 0:01:25] } Kxf7 { [%clk 0:01:49] } 27. Bxd4 { [%clk 0:01:24] As the dust settles it seems we simplified while up material so a great result for us. Opposite bishops, drawish, but perhaps Aman has a tactical idea.... } e3+ { [%clk 0:01:44] Ouch. } 28. Kxe3 { [%clk 0:01:16] } Bxb1 { [%clk 0:01:40] } 29. a4 { [%clk 0:01:06] } Bc2 { [%clk 0:01:37] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

