{ https://youtu.be/2WhtInYXPio?si=9PpnL_BozwBZDop_&t=2250 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. Nc3 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Nf3 { [%clk 0:05:00] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } (3... d4 4. Nb5 e5 (4... c5 5. a4 Nc6 6. Bc4 Bd7 7. d3 a6 8. Na3 Bd6 9. O-O Nge7 10. Bb3 O-O 11. Bd2 { This is another set up the engine likes. }) 5. Nxe5 a6 6. Na3 Qe7 7. f4 Nd7 8. Nf3 Qxe4+ 9. Qe2 Qxe2+ 10. Bxe2 { The engine wants a line like this. }) 4. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } (4. e5 d4 5. Ne2 c5) 4... exd5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 5. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:54] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:53] Again, an early Nf6, before Bd6, c6. } 6. d4 { [%clk 0:04:53] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:04:52] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:48] } 8. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:50] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 9. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:47] } Qe8 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 10. f4 { [%clk 0:04:45] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 11. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:41] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 12. Bh5 { [%clk 0:04:35] "an interesting move from our opponent. A good move, but probably not played for the right reasons" unfortunately Aman did not expound on what the right reasons were. Presumably provoke the g pawn push and take advantage of the weakened dark squares? } g6 { [%clk 0:04:39] } 13. Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:33] } Bxg4 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 14. Qxg4 { [%clk 0:04:31] Here Aman ponders how best to proceed in "this beautiful position" presumably being sarcastic. It seems we are hitting the limits of the stonewall as black against e4 at this rating range and wins from here on out may more than usual be down to Aman's GM skills enabling him to convert bad positions into wins. } Rf5 { [%clk 0:03:35] Aman considered Qc8 here (offering a trade). He was concerned about the f5 pawn push though. In the end he was baited by the chat into playing Rf5. } 15. Bh6 { [%clk 0:04:08] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:03:31] Again following the chat's recommendation, or at least that's the bit. } 16. h4 { [%clk 0:03:48] Aman was expecting Qh4 and was contemplating Rh5 in response. } Qe6 { [%clk 0:03:04] baiting h5. Looks like a tremendous move. g pawn is pinned and cannot take. Rxh5 drops the queen. But we will respond rxe5 here winning a piece as Qxe6 is met by Rxe6. } 17. Rae1 { [%clk 0:03:08] } Rxe5!! { [%clk 0:02:56] This still works. Only move from the opponent was to play Qh3 or something, allowing for fxe5 and on Qxh3 the pawn can recapture gxh3. } 18. fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:53] } Qxg4 { [%clk 0:02:53] } 19. exd6 { [%clk 0:02:52] } Qxh4 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 20. Be3 { [%clk 0:02:48] } Rf8 { [%clk 0:02:27] } 21. Rxf8+ { [%clk 0:02:46] } Kxf8 { [%clk 0:02:21] } 22. Rf1+ { [%clk 0:02:44] } Ke8 { [%clk 0:02:17] Interesting that Aman elects for Ke8 here. Perhaps centralizing the king getting ready for the end game? } 23. Bf4 { [%clk 0:02:37] Aman was hoping for Rf4 which would be met with Ke1+ and presumably Qxe3+ winning the bishop. } Qf6 { [%clk 0:01:48] Getting the knight to g4 here looks good but actually there are no mate threats. So Aman elects for this move. } 24. Be5 { [%clk 0:02:26] "I was hoping he would play that". Simplifying into a winning endgame. } Qxf1+ { [%clk 0:01:36] Simplifying into a winning endgame. } 25. Kxf1 { [%clk 0:02:25] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:01:35] Simplifying into a winning endgame. } 26. dxe5 { [%clk 0:02:24] } Kd7 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 27. Kf2 { [%clk 0:02:22] } Ke6 { [%clk 0:01:34] } 28. g4 { [%clk 0:02:19] } h5 { [%clk 0:01:32] } 29. gxh5 { [%clk 0:02:14] } gxh5 { [%clk 0:01:32] Creating two past pawns, the dreaded "trousers". (RIP Danya). } 30. b3 { [%clk 0:02:12] } h4 { [%clk 0:01:31] } 31. c4 { [%clk 0:02:10] } h3 { [%clk 0:01:27] } 32. cxd5+ { [%clk 0:02:08] } cxd5 { [%clk 0:01:26] } 33. Kg3 { [%clk 0:02:06] } e3 { [%clk 0:01:25] } 34. Kxh3 { [%clk 0:02:06] } e2 { [%clk 0:01:23] } 35. Kg2 { [%clk 0:02:06] } e1=Q { [%clk 0:01:22] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

