{ https://youtu.be/2WhtInYXPio?si=TAr6NUCLVRe1TyV-&t=2738 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] "he could be one of us..." } e6 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:56] "something tells me he's one of us" } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 5. f4 { [%clk 0:04:53] wonstaller confirmed. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 6. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:51] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:39] } 7. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:49] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 8. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:45] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 9. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:44] } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:04:18] } 10. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:42] Imbalance is that thanks to our wonestall brethren not castling, we have the open f file. Aman looking at Qg5, Qh4 as follow ups. } Qg5 { [%clk 0:03:50] } 11. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:39] } b6 { [%clk 0:03:48] with Ba6 ideas. } 12. Rf1 { [%clk 0:04:36] } Rxf1+ { [%clk 0:03:41] setting up a tactic } 13. Kxf1 { [%clk 0:04:33] } Ba6 { [%clk 0:03:38] } 14. c4 { [%clk 0:04:28] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:03:28] Developing the knight with ideas to active the rook. } 15. b3 { [%clk 0:04:23] } Nb4 { [%clk 0:03:18] Ba3 falls to Nc2 } 16. Kg1 { [%clk 0:04:03] } c5 { [%clk 0:02:40] Aman considered Nc2 here but the opponent has Nxe4 in response, creating a double threat on the queen and the knight as an attack on the knight by the queen is discovered. Still Ba3 by our opponent doesn't work, but if we play Rf8 it becomes playable. (the bishop would skewer the rook pinning the knight and preventing our Nc2 response).
Nc2 works after Ba3 because then Nxe4 in response falls to Exe3+ where Qxe3 is met by Nxe3. (we would eventually win the bishop in this line 17. Ba3 Nc2 18. Nxe4 Qxe3+ 19. Qxe3 Nxe3 20. cxd5 Nc2 21. Rd1 exd5 22. Nd6 Nxa3
This c pawn push closes the diagonal to the f8 square so in the future we would be able to swing the rook over to f8 without worrying about Ba3 in response. } 17. Nf1 { [%clk 0:03:52] } Rf8 { [%clk 0:02:01] Aman considered cxd4 but his concern was exd4 then opens up the opponent's bishop. (and a discovered attack on the queen). } 18. Ba3 { [%clk 0:03:45] } Nd3 { [%clk 0:01:56] guarding the c pawn. The position here is very equal but the imbalance is we have a better knight and rook, ownership of the f file, so we have a superior position. Our queen is also more active than his. The bishop is arguably also better because of the pieces it's aiming at. There is also pressure on the opponents c pawn, and Rf2 ideas, so lots for us to be happy about in this position. } 19. dxc5 { [%clk 0:03:13] } Rf2 { [%clk 0:01:08] Qxf2 is actually forced because otherwise Rxg2, Nf2 leads to mate. } 20. Qd1 { [%clk 0:03:00] } Rxg2+ { [%clk 0:01:04] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

