{ https://youtu.be/2WhtInYXPio?si=O6ThH3RnFzpQByBc&t=4114 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:59] Aman comments here that if we see Nf3 it is likely we are going into the French exchange. On d4 more likely is the advanced. } d5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. d4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:52] Funny how often f5 puts the opponent in the think thank. "it's a very strange move". } (4... Bd6 5. Bd3 Ne7 6. O-O Nbc6 7. Bg5 f6 8. Bh4 Bg4 9. h3 Bh5 10. Be2 Qd7 11. Bg3 O-O-O 12. Bxd6 Qxd6 { How the French exchange aggressive variation may have played out. }) 5. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:43] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:50] Again, knight out before bishop when playing black. } 6. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:39] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 7. Qe2+ { [%clk 0:04:35] "this is like the anti- me wanting-to-keep-the-queens-on move." } Qe7 { [%clk 0:04:36] Queen off the board is not really a major issue. We can still go for our Ne4 plan. } 8. Bxf6 { [%clk 0:04:34] } Qxe2+ { [%clk 0:04:31] } 9. Bxe2 { [%clk 0:04:31] } gxf6 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 10. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:29] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 11. a3 { [%clk 0:04:27] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:19] The beginning of our maneuvers. Aman points out that the knight HAS to go to e4 via d6 (currently occupied by the bishop) because the f6 square is occupied by a pawn. So, we need to figure out how to move the bishop, re-route the knight (Nb6, Nc4, Nd6, Ne4 maybe?? No, we will also move the LSB and go Nb6, Nc8, Nd6 maybe?). } 12. O-O-O { [%clk 0:04:25] } Nb6 { [%clk 0:04:09] Planning to move the LSB and play Nc8 } 13. Nh4 { [%clk 0:03:58] Bh5+ ideas. Aman considers it annoying but elects to allow it to happen. } Bd7 { [%clk 0:03:49] } 14. Bh5+ { [%clk 0:03:54] Aman was more concerned about Bd3 putting pressure on the doubled pawns. } Kf8 { [%clk 0:03:44] Our f5 pawn is not in danger which is a small victory. } 15. Rhe1 { [%clk 0:03:38] } Rd8 { [%clk 0:03:42] With Nc8 ideas. Followed by moving the DSB and Night to d6, e4. Opponent needs to find a way to attack the f pawn but Aman does not see an easy way to do it. Nc4 also an option with the advantage it looks like we are attacking something else and obscuring our true motive to route to e4. } 16. Re2 { [%clk 0:02:57] } Rg8 { [%clk 0:03:34] The plan is still Ne4 but this is presumably played as a preparatory move to attack the g pawn and keep the knight on h4 and indirectly defend the f pawn before proceeding with the main plan? } 17. Rde1 { [%clk 0:02:52] } Rg5 { [%clk 0:03:22] Rf2 was tempting, but Nxf5, Bxf5 and Re8+, Rxe8, Rxe8# would be nasty. This move aims to boot the bishop. } 18. Bf3 { [%clk 0:02:38] } Nc4 { [%clk 0:02:44] Aman wants to play Bc7 but he's concerned about Re7. Opponent can't play b3 to boot the knight (it would drop the a pawn) so it's quite a pleasant outpost. } 19. g3 { [%clk 0:01:46] "kind of expected that move and it's pretty good". } Rg7 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 20. Ng2 { [%clk 0:01:40] } Bc7 { [%clk 0:01:46] We can play this now because the rook on g7 covers the infiltration. } 21. Nf4 { [%clk 0:01:25] } Bxf4+ { [%clk 0:01:42] Not sure why Aman made this trade. He gives up the bishop pair and allows the opponent to put a pawn on f4. It is the top engine move, however. } 22. gxf4 { [%clk 0:01:23] } Nd6 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 23. Na4 { [%clk 0:01:11] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:01:33] This is what Jeremy Silman spoke about in Amateur Mind about the importance of having and sticking to a plan, and finding a way to (by digging deep) to impose your will/ plan on your opponent. The Ne4 idea has been our North Star all game. We never got distracted by side quests although we needed to temporarily pause to deal with tactical considerations and shut down counterplay. But through it all the goal was always in mind and was achieved by sheer force of will. } 24. Nc5 { [%clk 0:01:06] } Bc8 { [%clk 0:01:31] Saving the bishop which dominates the knight from that square two squares removed on the same file and covers the important Ne6+ jump. Interesting Aman does not seem interested in trading this bad bishop, however. He often likes to go into NvB endgames but perhaps he wants to encourage Nxe4 maybe? } 25. Bh1 { [%clk 0:00:49] After all our hard work it looks like our non cooperative opponent plans to kick the knight with f3. } b6 { [%clk 0:01:22] } 26. Nd3 { [%clk 0:00:47] } a5 { [%clk 0:01:08] our idea now switches to pawn storm attack maybe? } 27. f3 { [%clk 0:00:38] Kicking us out, yes, but the bishop on h1 is looking mighty sad. } Nd6 { [%clk 0:00:54] } 28. b3 { [%clk 0:00:32] } Ba6 { [%clk 0:00:47] } 29. Kd2 { [%clk 0:00:27] } Re8 { [%clk 0:00:40] Aman seems to be looking to simplify, get the rooks off the board, considering we have the imbalance of a more active bishop } 30. Rxe8+ { [%clk 0:00:26] } Nxe8 { [%clk 0:00:40] } 31. Re6 { [%clk 0:00:24] } Bb7 { [%clk 0:00:35] protecting the c pawn. } 32. h4 { [%clk 0:00:13] } Ba6 { [%clk 0:00:29] Aman baits the pawn capture here. } 33. h5 { [%clk 0:00:10] } Kf7 { [%clk 0:00:21] He's not taking the pawn so "we'll have to force him to take it". He wants to trap the bishop with Rg1 which right now would be met with Re1. But Aman has a blind spot in that Rxc6, Bxd3, cxd3 (not Kxd3) and on Rg8 the opponent now has Rc8 holding everything together. } 34. Rxc6 { [%clk 0:00:08] "there we go". } Bxd3 { [%clk 0:00:20] } 35. cxd3 { [%clk 0:00:06] } Rg1 { [%clk 0:00:19] } 36. Rc1 { [%clk 0:00:05] It seems Aman missed this Rc1 resource. Actually stockfish says that Rxc1 is still winning. Presumably it drops the opponent's king back meaning we get to the middle first, and also his Bishop remains trapped, giving us the edge in the NvB endgame. } Rg3?? { [%clk 0:00:13] A rare blunder. } 37. Re1 { [%clk 0:00:03] } Nc7 { [%clk 0:00:08] } 38. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:02] } Ne6 { [%clk 0:00:07] } 39. Kf2 { [%clk 0:00:01] } Rh3 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 40. Bg2 { [%clk 0:00:00] } Rxh5 { [%clk 0:00:03] 0-1 Black wins on time. Aman wins on time but what a game by our opponent. ggs. } 0-1

