{ https://youtu.be/jzAMNO9Z6KU?si=QHyiH5IZRSa_zWln&t=15 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:54] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. c4 { [%clk 0:04:49] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:48] threatening e4 } f5 { [%clk 0:04:56] stopping e4 } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:46] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:55] Once again we see the pawn structure set up before any pieces are developed with black. } 5. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:44] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:54] immediately challenging the bishop and pre-moving recapture by the queen. } 6. e3 { [%clk 0:04:43] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 7. Rc1 { [%clk 0:04:42] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:46] } 8. Qb3 { [%clk 0:04:40] a non threat. } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 9. cxd5 { [%clk 0:04:35] presumably our opponent wants to open lines and focus on a queen side attack. } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:26] But we are happy to get rid of our e pawn weakness. } 10. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:19] unprincipled and falls to the backwards knight capture. } Nxg5 { [%clk 0:04:23] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

