{ https://youtu.be/jzAMNO9Z6KU?si=q5YhLTELk1O8OcoV&t=471 } 1. Nc3 { [%clk 0:05:00] "an interesting first move" } d5 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:51] he goes d4 so will take the opportunity to play f5. } 3. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:48] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:48] Aman says he likes to go c6 first here because Nb5 is sometimes a threat. e6 is planned as a follow up. } 4. e3 { [%clk 0:04:45] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:46] And again we have the pawn structure set up before any pieces are developed with black. } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:43] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 6. Bxd6 { [%clk 0:04:41] } Qxd6 { [%clk 0:04:43] We are weaker on the dark squares now and don't love to trade our DSB, but later in the video Aman explains that our opponent's DSB is actually a strong piece when outside of the pawn chain, so it's a fairly equal trade. } 7. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:40] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:41] to prevent Nd5. } 8. a3 { [%clk 0:04:37] } Ngf6 { [%clk 0:04:40] with O-O and Ne4 planned as follow ups. Here Aman notes that while we have control of the e4 square, our opponent doesn't quite have control of e5 for himself. } 9. h3 { [%clk 0:04:06] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:37] } 10. O-O { [%clk 0:03:57] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 11. Ne2 { [%clk 0:03:52] One of the first times we've seen Ne2 played. On Nf4 we could respond with g5. } Rf6 { [%clk 0:03:44] 0-1 Black wins on time. Aman considers e5 but elects for this move. The engine likes e5 more. Aman also explains that Nf6 here would be a "typical bad move in the stonewall" as it would allow Ne5 then f3 to boot our knight. You would lose your e4 and e5 squares with one move.
Opponent abandons the game here. A shame, would have liked to have seen what Aman's ideas for the rook uplift were. } 0-1

