{ https://youtu.be/jzAMNO9Z6KU?si=TAT4co-2N_I-mUWI&t=2050 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 4. c4 { [%clk 0:04:58] Aman is impressed with this move which we've not seen so far. } c6 { [%clk 0:04:42] Best we can do in response, according to Aman. } (4... Nf6 5. Nc3 c6 6. cxd5 cxd5 7. Nf3 Bd6 8. Bg5 O-O 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 10. Nxd5 Re8+ 11. Be2 Qe6 12. Ne3 Bf4 13. O-O Bxe3 14. fxe3 Qxe3+ 15. Kh1 Qxe2 16. Qxe2 Rxe2 17. Rfe1 Rxe1+ 18. Rxe1 Be6 19. d5 Bd7 20. Re7 Kf8 21. d6 f6 22. Nd4 a5 23. g4 Ra6 24. Ne6+ Bxe6 25. Rxe6 Nd7 26. g5 Nc5 27. Re1 Rxd6 { Have the French exchange, conservative variation, may have played out in our favor. }) 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } f5?? { [%clk 0:04:25] Aman considered Bd6 here but elects for the f pawn push, again completing pawn structure before developing pieces with black. } 6. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:53] } (6. cxd5 cxd5 7. Bb5+ Bd7 8. Bxd7+ Nxd7 9. O-O Bd6 10. Re1+ Ne7 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. Ne6 Qd7 13. Nxg7+ Kf7 14. Nh5 { One way the SW vs e4 can go terribly wrong if our opponent knows what they're doing. }) 6... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:23] Knight before bishop here. Perhaps a good habit as it coveres the h5 square to prevent the queen check (although not possible here) but also perhaps to remain flexible with the bishop which may be needed on e7 to block a check? It's confusing because many times Aman plays bishop first but with black that seems to be done to challenge our opponent's bishop. With white we mainly play 3. Bd3 so one difference between playing the stonewall as black vs white seems to be that we often set up the pawn structure before developing pieces with black, and we often play Nf6 before Bd6 with black. Perhaps being a tempo down demands that we maintain greater piece flexibility with black and especially the black bishop which may go to e7 or d6 depending on our opponents moves. } 7. Bg5 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 8. Qe2+ { [%clk 0:04:49] } Qe7 { [%clk 0:04:12] the engine certainly wants the queen trade here. } 9. c5 { [%clk 0:04:47] } Qxe2+ { [%clk 0:04:06] } 10. Bxe2 { [%clk 0:04:45] } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:06] interestingly not Bc7. Perhaps to discourage the Bxf6 capture of our stonewall knight, (and avoid doubled pawns on the f file) and also the bishop on the b8-h7 diagonal is not as strong with queens off the board? Aman doesn't explain why. } (10... O-O 11. cxd6 { losing the bishop }) 11. O-O { [%clk 0:04:39] } Nbd7 { [%clk 0:03:57] } 12. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:38] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:03:53] } 13. Bxe7 { [%clk 0:04:35] } Kxe7 { [%clk 0:03:53] } 14. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:30] } Kf6 { [%clk 0:03:41] activating our king and with the DSBs traded off and no queens our king should be safe here. Also covers Ng5. } 15. Rfe1 { [%clk 0:04:24] } g5 { [%clk 0:03:31] I wondered about Re8 challenging for the open file but the engine likes Aman's immediate g5 or else it gives our opponent time to play h4 preventing g5. } 16. Re2 { [%clk 0:04:23] } g4 { [%clk 0:03:21] } 17. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:20] Seems like a big idea in the stonewall is to beat the opponent's knights back. As Silman says in AM "the way to play against knights is to deny them advanced support points". Once we have accomplished this and Ne5 is no longer a threat it allows us to move our d7 knight which in turn frees up our LSB and then our a8 rook. } Nf8 { [%clk 0:03:11] With Ne6 ideas. } 18. Rae1 { [%clk 0:04:18] } Ne6 { [%clk 0:03:09] } 19. f3 { [%clk 0:04:09] Aman was expecting Nb3 to defend the d pawn. } Nxd2 { [%clk 0:02:15] A complicated position. Aman considered Nxd4, fxe4, Nxe2, Rxe2, fxe4 which the engine thinks is winning for black by around -1.6. But he decided that taking on d2, f3 and d4 are more principled and "what you're supposed to do". } (19... Nxd4 20. fxe4 Nxe2+ 21. Rxe2 fxe4) 20. Rxd2 { [%clk 0:04:07] } gxf3 { [%clk 0:02:13] } 21. Rf1 { [%clk 0:04:00] Aman was expecting gxf3 with Rg8 in response but this move seemed to surprise him. } Nxd4 { [%clk 0:01:59] Completing the plan, which the engine has being about equal to the alternate line that started with an immediate Nxd4. } 22. gxf3 { [%clk 0:03:55] } Rg8+ { [%clk 0:01:47] we seem to have transposed to the line Aman had in mind but with a white rook on f1. I think the opponent's move was smart because without it Aman would have had Nxf3 forking the king and one of the rooks. } 23. Kh1 { [%clk 0:03:53] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:01:33] } 24. Ne2 { [%clk 0:03:50] } Nxe2 { [%clk 0:01:20] Aman noted concerns that the LSB could move here but said he "did not feel compelled to deal with it just yet". } 25. Rxe2 { [%clk 0:03:48] } Rae8 { [%clk 0:01:17] } 26. Rfe1 { [%clk 0:03:47] } Rxe2 { [%clk 0:01:15] } 27. Rxe2 { [%clk 0:03:47] } a5 { [%clk 0:01:09] engine evaluates black as completely winning here, -3.5 or so. I suppose the queen side pawn majority and greater king activity (actually, our opponents king is in jail) must be the imbalances giving us such an advantage. Also it seems Aman is pushing his queenside pawn majority which also placing pawns on dark squares to make them impervious to the LSBs attacks. } 28. f4 { [%clk 0:03:24] seems like an OK move to fix blacks pawn on a light square and shut down the king but the engine doesn't like it. } Rg4 { [%clk 0:01:05] and this seems to be why f4 was a bad move for white. } 29. Rf2 { [%clk 0:03:21] } Be6 { [%clk 0:01:02] bad bishops protect good pawns. RIP Danya. } 30. h3 { [%clk 0:03:20] } Rg3 { [%clk 0:01:01] fork } 31. Bc2 { [%clk 0:03:13] } Rxh3+ { [%clk 0:00:56] } 32. Kg2 { [%clk 0:03:13] } Rh4 { [%clk 0:00:54] winning a pawn with the disadvantage we let the opponent's king out of jail. But Aman is a known pawn grabber and the game is close enough that a pawn is a significant material advantage. Black is -4.6 here according to the engine. } 33. Kg3 { [%clk 0:03:11] } Rg4+ { [%clk 0:00:54] } 34. Kf3 { [%clk 0:03:10] } d4 { [%clk 0:00:53] with ideas of Bd5 } 35. b3 { [%clk 0:03:08] } Bd5+ { [%clk 0:00:50] } 36. Ke2 { [%clk 0:03:06] } Be4 { [%clk 0:00:49] simplifying } 37. Bd1 { [%clk 0:03:00] } h5 { [%clk 0:00:39] Interesting. I thought about simplifying with a trade of rooks Rg2, Rxg2, Bxg2 but the evaluation is pretty much a draw (slight -0.5 edge for black) after this. It seems our opponents greater king activity along with same colored bishops would be enough to hold the game with the rooks off the board. This is why end game study is so critical to middle game success, I guess. } (37... Rg2 38. Rxg2 Bxg2) 38. Kd2 { [%clk 0:02:59] } h4 { [%clk 0:00:37] wow, h4!
Giving up the exchange to create connected outside passed pawns.
Rooks are terrible against connected passed pawns. } 39. Bxg4 { [%clk 0:02:57] } fxg4 { [%clk 0:00:36] } 40. Re2 { [%clk 0:02:53] } h3 { [%clk 0:00:30] h3! The bishop is irrelevant. } 41. Ke1 { [%clk 0:02:46] } g3 { [%clk 0:00:27] } 42. Kf1 { [%clk 0:02:44] } Bd3 { [%clk 0:00:26] 0-1 Black wins. nasty. } 0-1

