{ https://youtu.be/Dd7dylcPXP0?si=1P7thU0mGKP5W11F&t=13 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4. e3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:48] Interesting Aman goes for the pawn set up before pieces, not even challenging the bishop, here too, against the London. Possibly because he knows the opponent is using a system opening so we will have time, especially with the closed position, to bring out the pieces later. } 5. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:50] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:47] And now we see bishop before knight. Perhaps to immediately challenge the strong London bishop. Also, Qh5+ not a threat. } 6. Bxd6 { [%clk 0:04:46] } Qxd6 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 7. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:45] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 8. c3 { [%clk 0:04:41] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:40] } 9. f4 { [%clk 0:04:40] why did Aman allow this? Could he not have played Nd7 before Nf6 or O-O? He doesn't really explain but he often seems to accept that Ne5 and F4 can't really be prevented when playing black. } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:35] going for our special maneuver with the LSB. Aman doesn't explain why but perhaps when our opponent gets a strong knight outpost on e5 it becomes more of an idea? We have time to do the maneuver perhaps, and maybe we could even tempt Nxd7 from our opponent? } 10. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:38] } Be8 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 11. O-O { [%clk 0:04:37] } Nbd7 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 12. Qe1 { [%clk 0:04:27] } h6 { [%clk 0:04:25] Aman indicates that Kh7 is a future idea. We don't want to capture the knight of course otherwise we will be forked. } 13. Rf3 { [%clk 0:04:22] } Bh5 { [%clk 0:04:21] } 14. Rg3 { [%clk 0:04:19] } Bxe2 { [%clk 0:04:19] we are very happy with this trade. } 15. Qxe2 { [%clk 0:04:18] } Kh7 { [%clk 0:04:15] avoiding the "red dot" rook on G3 perhaps? Rg8 is a follow up idea. Aman is also thinking about g5 but is not quite ready to play it. Also he wants to capture the knight but "not quite yet". } 16. Rg6 { [%clk 0:04:07] A "funny looking move" threatens nothing. And, after Nxe5 the rook is hanging. } Qe7 { [%clk 0:04:04] Getting ready for Nxe5 (so there will be no pawn fork in response). } 17. h4 { [%clk 0:04:04] Opponent probably had to move the rook back here. } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:59] } 18. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:00] } Kxg6 { [%clk 0:03:58] And we win the exchange. } 19. exf6 { [%clk 0:03:56] } gxf6 { [%clk 0:03:53] Taking back with the pawn gxf6 to keep the other knight from coming into e5. Plus we get a semi open g file for our rook(s). } 20. Rf1 { [%clk 0:03:44] } Rg8 { [%clk 0:03:51] } 21. Kh2 { [%clk 0:03:36] } Kh7 { [%clk 0:03:47] } 22. Qh5 { [%clk 0:03:31] } Qd6+ { [%clk 0:03:32] Rg4 here would have lost to Rxf5 leaving our rook loose. } 23. Kh1 { [%clk 0:03:26] } Rg6 { [%clk 0:03:28] With ideas of Rag8 to double the rooks. } 24. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:22] } Rag8 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 25. Rf2 { [%clk 0:03:20] } Qg3 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 26. Re2 { [%clk 0:03:12] } R8g7 { [%clk 0:03:06] stopping Qf7+ in case the g6 rook moves. } 27. Kg1 { [%clk 0:03:04] } Rg4 { [%clk 0:03:02] } 28. Kh1 { [%clk 0:03:02] } b6 { [%clk 0:02:47] No further progress seems possible on the king side. Aman switches his attention to the queenside, looking to create a passed pawn and queen or create an open file for the rook to swing across and threaten infiltration. } 29. b3 { [%clk 0:02:59] } c5 { [%clk 0:02:46] } 30. a3 { [%clk 0:02:47] } a5 { [%clk 0:02:42] } 31. b4 { [%clk 0:02:42] } cxb4 { [%clk 0:02:37] } 32. cxb4 { [%clk 0:02:39] } Rc7 { [%clk 0:02:33] switching to the open file on the queenside. Qf7+ still not possible. } 33. Ne1 { [%clk 0:02:26] defending Rc1+ but leaving the h pawn undefended. } Rxh4+ { [%clk 0:02:27] 0-1 Black wins. fork. } 0-1

