{ https://youtu.be/DSvi3ngZr0A?si=KwfDp4sGQKzjvqRd&t=3131 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:59] Seems we are up against the London. } e6 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:53] "Usually I like to establish Bd6 here". No you don't!! But perhaps he means "against the London" by "here"? } 4. Bg3 { [%clk 0:04:55] Probably learned his London from Gotham. } f5 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:53] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:48] Next, on c4, c6, otherwise we look to castle. } 6. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:38] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:46] } 7. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:30] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 8. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:20] } fxe4 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 9. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:19] Aman is so much more likely to allow Ne5 with black than Ne4 from black when playing white. With white we would have played Nd2 before playing the stonewall knight jump into e5. Not sure why but I am guessing playing a tempo down forces the urgency? } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:30] we challenge the knight. } 10. f4 { [%clk 0:04:03] Plays f4 but the problem for the opponent is he's doing it after our knight leap. So, we have a pawn on e4 and can take en passant. } exf3 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 11. Ndxf3 { [%clk 0:04:01] Opponent sets up to replace the e5 knight with a knight. But, even so, Nxe5 might be a good move here for us. Because Nxe5 in response would leave the f file open to an uncastled king and prevent our opponent castling. } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:53] Nf6 with Ne4 ideas were also tempting but playing for a position where our opponent's king can't castle proves the more tempting option. } 12. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:00] } b6 { [%clk 0:03:17] Qg5 was also an option here. Aman decides against it because it "would give him Bf4 which would be giving him a tempo perhaps we don't need to provide".
b6 is played with Ba6 ideas. (perhaps after a5)
Ba6 would stop our opponent challenging for the open file with Rf1. } 13. Qg4 { [%clk 0:03:30] stops Ba6 as it would drop the e pawn to Qxe6. } Qf6 { [%clk 0:02:50] Guarding the e pawn. If Nd7 we have Qf5. } 14. O-O-O { [%clk 0:03:25] } Ba6 { [%clk 0:02:48] Opponent cannot challenge for the f file now. } 15. Nd7 { [%clk 0:03:18] } Qf5 { [%clk 0:02:47] } 16. Qxf5 { [%clk 0:02:49] } Rxf5 { [%clk 0:02:44] } 17. Ne5 { [%clk 0:02:30] } Raf8 { [%clk 0:02:41] } 18. Rd2 { [%clk 0:02:18] } h5 { [%clk 0:01:58] Before playing this, Aman comments that it is very important to leave his c pawn on c7, or else our opponent would move the knight and force a trade of bishops.
